problems.problem:
Let $A$ be an $m \times n$ matrix $(m \ne n)$ with entries $0$ and $1$. Suppose that for every injective map $f : \{1, 2, \dots, m\} \to \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$ there exists $i$ with $1 \le i \le m$ such that the $(i, f(i))$ entry of $A$ is $0$. Prove that there exist $S \subseteq \{1, 2, \dots, m\}$ and $T \subseteq \{1, 2, \dots, n\}$ such that:

(i) the $(i, j)$ entry is $0$ for all $i \in S$, $j \in T$;

(ii) $|S| + |T| > n$.

Let $ABCDE$ be a convex pentagon. Let $S$ and $R$ be the intersections of $BE$ with $AC$ and $AD$ respectively, let $T$ and $P$ be the intersections of $BD$ with $CA$ and $CE$ respectively, and let $Q$ be the intersection of $CE$ and $AD$. The triangles $ASR$, $BTS$, $CPT$, $DQP$, $ERQ$ all have area $1$. Find the areas of

(1) the pentagon $PQRST$;

(2) the pentagon $ABCDE$.

Given an integer $n \ge 3$, prove that there exists a set $A_n$ with the following two properties:

(1) $A_n$ consists of $n$ distinct positive integers;

(2) for every $a \in A_n$, the product of all the other elements of $A_n$ leaves remainder $1$ upon division by $a$.

Find the largest integer $n$ for which one can place $n$ points in the plane, no three collinear, and colour each of them red, green or yellow so that:

(i) inside every triangle with all vertices red there is a green point;

(ii) inside every triangle with all vertices green there is a yellow point;

(iii) inside every triangle with all vertices yellow there is a red point.

Let $n$ be an odd positive integer. Prove that it is impossible to write exactly one of the numbers $1$, $2$, $3$ in each cell of a grid extending infinitely in all directions so that all of the following conditions hold:

(a) two cells sharing a side never contain the same number;

(b) no $3 \times 1$ or $1 \times 3$ block of consecutive cells contains $1, 2, 3$ in this order read from top to bottom, from bottom to top, from left to right, or from right to left;

(c) all $n \times n$ blocks of cells have the same sum, regardless of their position.

Let $T$ be a positive integer. Find all functions $f$ defined on ordered pairs of positive integers and taking positive integer values, together with integers $C_0, C_1, \dots, C_T$, such that:

• for every positive integer $n$, there are exactly $n$ pairs $(k, l)$ of positive integers with $f(k, l) = n$;

• for $t = 0, 1, \dots, T$ and every pair $(k, l)$ of positive integers, $f(k + t,\, l + T - t) - f(k, l) = C_t$.

Find all functions $f$ defined on the positive integers and taking positive integer values such that, for all positive integers $m$, $n$, the following two statements are equivalent:

• $n$ divides $m$;

• $f(n)$ divides $f(m) - n$.

In the $xy$-plane, a point whose $x$- and $y$-coordinates are both integers from $1$ to $2000$ is called a good point. For four points $A(x_1, y_1)$, $B(x_2, y_2)$, $C(x_3, y_3)$, $D(x_4, y_4)$ satisfying all of the following conditions, the polyline $ABCD$ is called a Z-polyline:

• $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ are all good points;

• $x_1 < x_2$ and $y_1 = y_2$;

• $x_2 > x_3$ and $y_2 - x_2 = y_3 - x_3$;

• $x_3 < x_4$ and $y_3 = y_4$.

Find the smallest positive integer $n$ for which there exist $n$ Z-polylines $Z_1, Z_2, \dots, Z_n$ satisfying the following condition:

for every good point $P$ there is an integer $i$ with $1 \le i \le n$ such that $P$ lies on $Z_i$.

Here the polyline $ABCD$ is the union of the segments $AB$, $BC$, $CD$ (all including their endpoints).

Find all polynomials $f(x)$ with integer coefficients such that, for every integer $n \ge 2$, both of the following hold:

• $f(n) > 0$;

• $f(n)$ divides $n^{f(n)} - 1$.

An acute triangle $ABC$ that is not isosceles contains three distinct points $A_1$, $B_1$, $C_1$ in its interior such that $AB_1 : CB_1 = AB : CB$ and $AC_1 : BC_1 = AC : BC$. Let $A_2$ be the reflection of $A_1$ in the line $BC$, $B_2$ the reflection of $B_1$ in the line $AC$, and $C_2$ the reflection of $C_1$ in the line $AB$. Suppose that all of the following hold:

• the four points $A$, $A_2$, $B$, $C_2$ lie on a circle;

• the four points $A$, $A_2$, $B_2$, $C$ lie on a circle;

• the four points $B$, $B_2$, $C$, $C_2$ lie on a circle;

• none of the three points $A_2$, $B_2$, $C_2$ lies on the circumcircle of triangle $ABC$.

Prove that triangles $A_1B_1C_1$ and $A_2B_2C_2$ are similar.
